WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:02.690
It is great to welcome back to the program
today.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:08.160
Dr. Isaac WBO, gosh, who's an infectious disease
physician and scientists based out of the

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.219
University of Toronto and Toronto General
Hospital.

00:00:12.219 --> 00:00:14.001
Always great to talk to you.

00:00:14.001 --> 00:00:16.580
Nice to see you again.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:24.470
So I guess just to start with briefly, we're
seeing the U.S. number of corona virus cases

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.950
down from their sort of second peak, still
around 40000 cases a day.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.220
I know you're in Canada.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.321
I don't know how closely you're following
the ins and outs of case numbers here, but

00:00:34.321 --> 00:00:39.010
there's some skepticism about whether these
case numbers can be trusted at this point,

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:45.010
given a lot of different things that have
gone on with the data, with testing pause

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.170
due to weather events and so many other things.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:54.010
My question to you is, is there really a better
metric to pay attention to at this point,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.420
be it hospitalizations or deaths or something
else?

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.500
In all fairness, I look at a combination of
metrics.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.009
I think the number of new cases per day is
very, very helpful.

00:01:04.009 --> 00:01:06.420
But above and beyond that, I look at other
metrics as well.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.130
I look at the seven day average.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.859
I look at the number of tests that are performed.

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:14.420
Look at the percentage of tests that are positive
out of the number of tests that are performed.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.130
And I really think that when we sort of combine
all of those metrics and more, we really paint

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.009
a more holistic picture that gives us a better
understanding.

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:27.430
Of course, if testing isn't being done, if
it's hard to know exactly what the ground

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:28.610
truth is.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:34.429
But in all fairness, I think it's by this
point in time, we sort of know in most regions

00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:39.240
how things are going and what direction things
are turning, either for better or for worse.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.849
So one specific question on testing and positivity.

00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:48.200
Sometimes we will see a situation where it
looks like in a particular state, cases have

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.270
gone down.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:57.169
And then when you look in a little more detail,
you see that tests are down 30 percent and

00:01:57.169 --> 00:02:03.200
new cases are only down 10 percent, which
implies a higher positivity rate.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.269
And it suggests that it's because you're doing
less testing, that cases have gone down.

00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:14.340
The counter point is, well, you know, if the
testing goes down, you're probably only testing

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.849
sicker people.

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:19.590
So it makes sense that the positivity rate
would go up.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.750
It doesn't necessarily mean there are actually
more cases out there.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.670
Is there any way to know for sure?

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.670
Well, yes, there is.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:34.750
And the real answer is getting lots of disparate
data sources together in one place.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.900
And really, that is why it is so important
to look at several metrics rather than just

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.150
relying on a handful of metrics.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.599
And then, of course, watching the metrics
through time, an individual day or even a

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:48.879
few days really does nothing to us.

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:54.200
It's really important to look at longer term
trends, seven day trends and beyond.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.599
And I think if we're starting to do that and
stop reacting to, you know, terms like uptick

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:05.060
and downtick, every time we see some variation
in the data, we'll be in a much better place.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.200
There's going to be the expected normal variations
on a day to day basis and even maybe on a

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.780
bit of a longer term basis.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:16.469
But if we look at weekly trends and look at
multiple data sources, I think we'll be in

00:03:16.469 --> 00:03:17.469
the right place.

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:21.269
And really, truly, all of those combined together
give us gives us a good impression.

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:25.780
I don't think anyone following this is really
looking at that one true metric that's really

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.140
going to provide all the answers.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.660
It's it's a multitude of metrics.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:34.520
So let's talk about the next three to four
months.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:40.530
Some of the countries that did successfully
flatten their curves are starting to see some

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.530
more cases.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.750
Spain is one example that I've been looking
at.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.109
And there are many others in the United States.

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:51.810
When we first got down to 20000 cases a day,
things just basically reopened and we went

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.690
all the way back up to 75.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.689
Now we're back down to 40.

00:03:55.689 --> 00:04:01.180
What is the best assessment now of what was
originally thought of in the May projections

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:07.530
to be a discrete second wave, which now is
just maybe going to be a kind of continuation?

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.390
What is the timing looking like?

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.659
How bad could it potentially get?

00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:16.950
It's interesting because we talk about the
second wave like it has some finite definition.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:20.700
And in all fairness, I don't think it really
matters whether or not this is a spike in

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.950
the first wave, a plateau in the first wave.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:27.360
But this thing, second wave, I think a lot
of this is irrelevant in the sense that, number

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.140
one, you still have a very susceptible population,
meaning that, you know, in most places, the

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.610
vast majority of people have not had this
infection so are susceptible to getting this

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.610
infection.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:43.160
And number two, if you don't have controls
in place to keep this infection at bay, you're

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.940
going to see widespread transmission.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:52.070
So, you know, if we look in the crystal ball
a lot, there's you know, if we can predict

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:57.980
what's going to happen, if people are in indoor
environments, in close proximity for prolonged

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:01.920
periods of time, we know that this is the
perfect setup for transmission.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.640
Now, let's think about what's going to happen
in the months ahead, at least in the northern

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:06.640
parts of the United States.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:07.780
It's going to get cool.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.360
We've got kids going back to school.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.330
We've got many people going back to work in
person.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.310
We've got cooler temperatures that are driving
us inside.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:21.900
We've got economies that are opening and all
of those just put people together in indoor

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.670
environments for prolonged periods of time.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:26.480
That is where this virus is transmitted.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.720
If we take steps to mitigate that, spread
people apart, put a mask on when you go inside,

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:33.640
limit the number of people in indoor settings.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:38.070
We can probably do a decent job in mitigating
the spread.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:41.780
But I still think we'll probably see a rise
in cases, at least in the northern part of

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:46.030
the United States and certainly in Canada
as we enter the fall and winter months.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:52.400
So in terms of masks, obviously there's been
a lot about masks over the last many months

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.420
in particular.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:06:00.710
There is disagreement, I guess we can say,
about the degree to which masks prevent transmission

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:05.530
and whether they're really to protect one
from spreading versus to protect one from

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:06.530
catching or both.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:10.490
There's a very popular meme that's been floating
around which assert, you know, it puts numbers

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.120
to it where it says both people wear a mask.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.400
It's like a one percent chance of transmission.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.210
If one person wears a masks it mask, it's
five percent.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.920
If neither one does, it's fit or whatever,
right?

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.600
Yeah, I'm sure those aren't the right numbers.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:29.310
But are there any numbers or specificity to
the effect of masks?

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:33.070
And then, of course, what type of mask, I'm
guessing is a factor as well.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.070
Right.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.170
I think we can talk about this in general
principles.

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:41.860
And, you know, I certainly just full disclosure,
mass is probably my least favorite topic of

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.880
this epidemic or pandemic.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:44.880
Interesting.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.800
For whatever reason, this was like the hot
button issue.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.970
This was politicized to such a degree.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:55.100
People have very, very strong opinions on
on many different sides of the equation.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.930
People are cherry picking data to suit their
preconceived ideas.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:03.290
And it's really frustrating when you really
want to just take a public health look at

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:07.280
this and epidemiologic look at it on medical
and scientific.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.280
Look at it.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.290
It's kind of frustrating.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:14.880
And quite frankly, it's frustrating because
people who should know better are are still,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.480
I think, speaking beyond the data or still
cherry picking data.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.520
Having said that, there are many rational
voices both in the lake community and them

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.960
and in the medical, scientific and public
health community.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.000
And I commend those people because anytime
we talk about this, for whatever reason, there

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.640
seems to be a dogpile effect.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.230
Now, let's get to the question.

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:40.030
So in general, based on what we know now,
we can talk about mass at an individual level

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:42.850
and we can also talk about masks at a population
level.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:48.060
I think we can also talk about mass in a health
care specific area as well.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.330
But in general, at an individual level, if
you put a mask on and you're infected, you're

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:56.220
just going to reduce the amount of virus that
you transmit to the environments around you.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:57.700
Seems to make sense.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:58.700
Pretty good idea.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:03.180
Now, does this pan out at a population level?

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.360
It's interesting.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.770
There's modeling studies, which quite frankly,
I know I work in epidemiology as well.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:11.900
And you know what?

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:15.980
My career is based on working with modeling
and whatnot.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:20.480
I've got to say, like, I should be the big
supporter of models.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:27.090
I think models are helpful, but I don't think
they really answer definitively the questions

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:28.530
that we want to answer.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:29.770
They're helpful.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:31.020
They're useful.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:34.690
But we have to take a lot of the modeling
data with a grain of salt.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.479
So at an individual level.

00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:37.479
Great.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:39.010
I think it's a no brainer.

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:40.930
We've known this long before Koban 19.

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:44.900
If you put on a mask, you just reduce the
amount of virus you're going to shed in the

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:47.970
environment around you at a population level.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.540
The waters get a bit murkier.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:55.540
Will it really help at a population level
if there's masking?

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:56.540
Yeah.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.400
You know, what is the end of the day?

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.510
If we sift through the data, it probably helps.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:07.550
But I think it helps to a much smaller extent
than what many people might believe or might

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:08.830
want to believe.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.370
But I'm not saying it doesn't help.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:11.370
I think it.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:15.940
And then, you know, how do you take this data
and translate into policy?

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:19.120
I think at the end of the day, we also know
where this virus is transmitted.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:24.230
It's almost, well, I should say exclusively,
but it's truly an indoor virus.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:30.180
Like, there's just remarkably few transmission
of this virus that are credible, that have

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.170
been documented in outdoor settings.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:37.560
Cherkin happened in an outdoor setting, but
it's just not nearly as common as indoor setting.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:43.330
So we take our data that we have with mass,
with the total appreciation that this is imperfect

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:47.300
data, with the total appreciation that it's
going to be impossible to know at this point

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:49.500
to have a perfect randomized double blind.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.850
Civil control trial or whatever we want to
design.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:55.740
But if we take the existing data that we have,
I think it's very reasonable to translate

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:00.490
that into policy, to say, listen, if you're
in an indoor setting, you should put on a

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:01.490
mask.

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:03.980
I think it's as simple as that outdoors.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:08.470
I mean, I've seen some of the narratives coming
out of the United States with people saying,

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:12.250
you know, I went for a 20 k jog and I had
a mask on my whole time.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:15.650
And then this didn't bother me one bit, you
know, mazeltov.

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:17.240
We're all very proud of you for wearing a
mask.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.470
But like, I don't think you're at risk of
transmitting this infection if you're on a

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:21.470
28 jog.

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:22.640
You probably don't need to worry about.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:23.860
I focus on indoor settings.

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:27.230
I think Masser a great idea taken the politics
out of it.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:29.350
We should be putting on a mask when we go
indoors.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:30.350
Yeah.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:33.150
I mean, I said we don't let's we'll move on
after this.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:37.130
But here in Boston now if you're just even
walking downtown, if you don't have a mask

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:41.200
on, you know, people can kind of look at you
from the studies I've looked at.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.930
It really doesn't seem like that is at all
a high risk scenario.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:47.860
But if people want to do it, like you said,
mazeltov, you know, like I am, I'll do it

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:48.860
all.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:52.030
I'll do it in order to participate, because
I don't want to be the person who is not participating.

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:55.220
But it doesn't really seem to be based too
much in the science.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.140
I think you have a really good point there.

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:04.400
And a lot of this bleeds well out of science
and medicine and into more sociological or

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.620
socio political conversation.

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:08.360
I'm sitting here in Toronto.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.870
I can not comment.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:15.150
I cannot empathize on what you're going through
in the United States in terms of the degree

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:17.650
of politicization that this has had.

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:24.310
And if the mask might also represent a lot
more than, hey, I'm I'm here to keep you safe

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:25.890
in case I'm infected.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:29.330
And I appreciate that there's a lot more going
on in the United States with regards to that

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:30.570
than there is in Canada.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:35.080
It might be a signal that I align with this
particular group or that I have these particular

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.080
beliefs.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:38.430
And in all fairness, I'm not into shaming
and blaming.

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.740
People want to wear a mask, go for it, wear
a mask.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:44.530
But like where I would be more of a stickler
about it is indoor setting.

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.380
I think it's important that people put them
on when they go inside.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:51.470
I think that will actually have some utility
in preventing the transmission of this infection.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:54.730
OK, so vaccines.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:12:01.380
Multiple vaccine candidates on spec production
of a number of them so that if indeed we learn

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.820
this one is a go or that one is a go.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:05.620
Doses will be available.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:07.170
Makes perfect sense.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:16.510
Russia claims to have a vaccine, which has
a number of questions around it at this point.

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:22.200
My thought has been the optimistic scenario
is somewhere around October 15, there's an

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:30.070
announcement made that a vaccine is safe and
at least gives four or five months of immunity.

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:32.750
It starts to be rolled out slowly.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:37.029
Health care workers maybe by the end of the
year, 2021.

00:12:37.029 --> 00:12:39.870
High risk populations.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.720
Is that still the optimistic scenario?

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.380
Where where are we on vaccines?

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:45.380
Yeah.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:48.380
I mean, the pace of vaccine development is
just staggering.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:49.870
This is phenomenal.

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:53.020
There's about two dozen that are in human
clinical trials.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:58.320
Remember, we know this infection existed seven
months ago and now seven months in.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.170
You've got just over two dozen human clinical
trials.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:05.380
I think right now there's six or seven that
are in what's called phase three human clinical

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:06.380
studies.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:07.380
That's amazing.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:11.180
I mean, that's that's where you're enrolling
thousands and thousands and thousands of people

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:12.180
to answer the question.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.630
Does it work?

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:14.630
And is it safe?

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:16.740
So, you know, there's there's tremendous pace.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:19.350
And of course, we know many of these vaccines
are going to fail.

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:22.750
But there's so many that are in development
and even in advanced stages of development

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:23.800
that I'm with you.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:30.690
I do share that optimistic outlook that we'll
probably have some successful trials in before

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:32.910
the end of 2020.

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:36.510
I wouldn't be surprised if needle start going
in arms before the end of 2020.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.120
Hey, I could be totally wrong.

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.089
I could be way off and it could be 20, 21,
and these might be a bust.

00:13:43.089 --> 00:13:48.160
But looking at the data that we have available
to us is pretty promising.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.490
It's not you know, nothing, of course, is
guaranteed.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:51.490
But I share that.

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:52.490
I share that view.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:14:01.220
I also like that concept where many governments,
or I guess in partnership with the companies

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:06.089
just say, you know what, let's just mass produce
these vaccines now.

00:14:06.089 --> 00:14:07.089
We appreciate that.

00:14:07.089 --> 00:14:10.279
We might be tossing a billion dollars out
the window if it doesn't work.

00:14:10.279 --> 00:14:11.279
But.

00:14:11.279 --> 00:14:14.760
The ones that do work, we can already start
to rollout vaccine programs.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.770
That's a really smart idea.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:17.800
You've got money to burn.

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:22.060
That's a great way to spend it, because I
think at the end of the day, when you balance

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:28.899
the economics of lost versus gained, I think
this will likely side towards a net gain.

00:14:28.899 --> 00:14:34.620
I'm not a health economics type of guy, but
I'm pretty sure that that's the way that the

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:37.540
tables will turn, or at least whatever the
word.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.070
You know what I'm trying to say?

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:44.310
Now, when it starts to become available, it
takes a while before we really get towards

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:47.680
being able to say we have ended the pandemic.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:48.680
Right.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:50.890
I mean, it's when you took my belief.

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:55.670
My understanding is with something as contagious
as coronavirus, about 70 percent immunity

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:57.790
is required for herd.

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:01.470
40 percent of the U.S. is not even interested
in getting the vaccine, which is a totally

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:02.870
different story.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:06.440
But this once the vaccine rolls out, it's
still going to be a while, right?

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:07.440
Yeah.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.440
Yeah, exactly.

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:11.730
I don't think this pandemic's going to end
with a bang, but more with a whimper.

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:12.730
Right.

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:17.140
It's probably going to peter out as more and
more people get vaccinated or as more and

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:22.110
more people get this infection and then recover
from it and have some degree of immunity for

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:23.800
some period of time.

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:25.430
So it's probably going to peter out.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:31.540
I think the other thing that it's not talked
about a lot is even though we'll have access

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:34.390
to this vaccine in many high income countries.

00:15:34.390 --> 00:15:38.120
This pandemic is still going to be raging
in many other parts of the world, especially

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:44.270
in low income countries that sadly are, you
know, might not have the same access to the

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:47.080
vaccine that you and I will have.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:51.700
And and I think there's going to be a lot
of individuals and a significant proportion

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:57.040
of the globe that will be suffering, disproportionately
suffering because of limited access to the

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:01.839
vaccine, because many of the high income countries
are buying it all up and snatching it all

00:16:01.839 --> 00:16:02.839
up.

00:16:02.839 --> 00:16:06.910
And and, you know, I really hope I know that
there are programs I know that WHL was looking

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:13.149
into this and there are groups looking into
this to really have more equitable vaccine

00:16:13.149 --> 00:16:14.149
programs worldwide.

00:16:14.149 --> 00:16:18.640
But, you know, part of me is a little bit
skeptical as to how successful those will

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:19.640
be.

